  "query_preview": "..Utah and southwest Wyoming remain under a south to\nsouthwest flow this evening which has brought unseasonably high\nmoisture to much of the area, particularly over southern Utah.\nSatellite derived PWs are in the 0.6 to 0.8 inch range over\nsouthern Utah and 0.3 to 0.5 inch elsewhere. This allowed for good\ncoverage of showers today, particularly along a line extending\nfrom southwest Utah through the Wasatch Front where some embedded\nshortwave energy moved through. \n\nThe remaining shortwave energy with exit to the north tonight,\ntaking some of the moisture with it. This will result in a\nrelative lull in precipitation on Saturday with just some\nscattered showers around. High temperatures are expected to stay\nnear seasonal normals across northern portions of the area, but in\nexcess of 5F below climo over southern Utah where more cloud cover\nis expected.\n\nConfidence remains high that ingredients will come together to\nbring widespread valley rain and mountain snow to Utah and\nsouthwest Wyoming Sunday into Monday. Southerly flow will bring up\nmoisture from a landfalling atmospheric river as a strong and\nprogressive Pacific storm system tracks across the state. With\nthis high amount of moisture and instability, rain will be heavy\nat times, creating a rather heightened potential for flash\nflooding, particularly for southern Utah. Going Flood Watch covers\nthis threat well. \n\nAs the strong cold front moves through, snow levels are expected\nto lower from 9,000 feet to as low as the 6,500 to 7,000 foot\nrange, bringing accumulating snow to all of Utah's mountains. \nThese totals could be significant, particularly for areas favored \nin northwest flow, and this has the potential to bring travel \ndifficulties to higher elevation routes. \n\nThe trough is expected to exit the area Monday morning, with just\na few mountain showers expected to linger into Monday afternoon.\nTemperatures will be quite unseasonably cool, with maxes averaging\n10-15F below seasonal normals. Weak transient high pressure is\nthen expected to keep conditions dry for Tuesday, but most\nguidance shows this being quickly pushed out of the way by yet\nanother Pacific trough Wednesday into Thursday, with an active\npattern then continuing through at least day seven. Ensemble\nspread remains high with regard to the timing, strength, and track\nof these individual systems so confidence is low with regard to\npotential impacts, but relatively high in the continued unsettled\npattern.",
